## Contract Anchoring

Every function must carry an `expect:` + `[P{N}]` contract annotation that links it to a code entity. This creates bidirectional traceability: code entity (`dcterms:identifier`) ↔ contract ↔ test ↔ implementation.

The anchor tag lives on the test and points at the code entity's qualified name (Dublin Core `dcterms:identifier`). The contract classifies the THING under test with Dublin Core (`dcterms:type` = SymbolKind, `dcterms:identifier` = qualified name, `dcterms:source` = file:line, `dcterms:subject` = MDS category derived from the entity's position in the code) and the FLOW with PKO (`pko:Procedure` = the call chain, `pko:Step` = each function, `pko:StepExecution` = the test execution, `pko:IssueOccurrence` = a test failure).

The contract format is:
```
/// expect: "<user's functional expectation in their own voice>"
/// [P{N}] Motivating: <principle name> — <why this contract exists>
/// pre:  <what must be true before calling>
/// post: <what the function guarantees after returning>
/// inv:  <what must always hold across operations> (optional, for types)
/// [P{N}] Constraining: <principle name> — <what the code cannot do>
/// prob: p=<threshold>, δ=<tolerance>, k=<recovery window> (only for non-deterministic functions per Testing Discipline §7.6)
```

